<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/5th-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sicilian Expedition</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">415–<a href="/years/413-bce">413 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> · Also known as Athenian expedition to Sicily · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sicilian Expedition was the Athenian invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a>, launched in 415 BCE on the pretext of aiding Segesta against Selinus and aimed at Syracuse. It ended in 413 BCE with the destruction of the Athenian fleet and army at Syracuse. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catania">Catana</a> served as an Athenian base, and the disaster badly weakened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sicilian-expedition.jpg" alt="Sicilian Expedition" width="320" height="474" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Steeple Davis · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Destruction_of_the_Athenian_army_at_Syracuse.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Peloponnesian War of 431–404 BCE Athens survived a devastating plague and won at Pylos in 425 BCE, but lost most of a vast armament in the <b>Sicilian Expedition</b> of 415–413 BCE, and Lysander's destruction of its last fleet at Aegospotami in 405 BCE forced surrender.</blockquote><a href="/empire/athens">Athens · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Thucydides blamed Athens’s defeat in the Peloponnesian War on leaders who, after Pericles, flattered the people, and on the Assembly’s enthusiasm for the <b>Sicilian Expedition</b> of 415–413 BCE, which ended in the loss of the fleet and army.</blockquote><a href="/government/direct-democracy">Direct democracy · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote>Plato, whose teacher Socrates was condemned to death by an Athenian jury in 399 BCE, compared the democratic city to a ship whose crew ignore the true navigator, and Thucydides blamed Athens's defeat by Sparta in part on the Assembly's enthusiasm for the <b>Sicilian Expedition</b>.</blockquote><a href="/essays/who-should-rule">Who Should Rule?</a></li><li><blockquote>It served as Athens' base during the <b>Sicilian Expedition</b> in 415 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/catana">Catana</a></li><li><blockquote>Its long border dispute with Selinous led it to seek Athenian help in 416 BCE, prompting the <b>Sicilian Expedition</b>, and then Carthaginian help, which ended in the destruction of Selinous in 409 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/segesta">Segesta</a></li></ul></section>
